1
00:00:00,090 --> 00:00:03,660
Léa: Hello everyone and welcome to The Cozy French.

2
00:00:03,700 --> 00:00:04,840
I'm Léa.

3
00:00:04,840 --> 00:00:06,760
Mathieu: And I'm Mathieu.

4
00:00:06,760 --> 00:00:09,920
Léa, why did you write "little bell"

5
00:00:09,920 --> 00:00:11,510
at the top of your notes?

6
00:00:11,560 --> 00:00:15,240
Léa: To remember my very first radio show.

7
00:00:15,240 --> 00:00:18,310
Mathieu: You needed a little bell to talk?

8
00:00:18,360 --> 00:00:20,220
Léa: I needed it to give

9
00:00:20,220 --> 00:00:23,720
the impression we were walking into a bakery.

10
00:00:23,720 --> 00:00:26,160
You'll see, it almost worked.

11
00:00:26,160 --> 00:00:28,100
Mathieu: Almost?

12
00:00:28,150 --> 00:00:28,890
Léa: When we listened back,

13
00:00:28,890 --> 00:00:30,440
it sounded like I had

14
00:00:30,440 --> 00:00:32,600
locked a bicycle in the studio.

15
00:00:32,600 --> 00:00:34,200
Mathieu: Ah, so that's why

16
00:00:34,200 --> 00:00:36,880
you wanted to talk to me about my bike.

17
00:00:36,880 --> 00:00:38,320
Léa: Among other things.

18
00:00:38,320 --> 00:00:40,600
Today, we're talking about that moment

19
00:00:40,600 --> 00:00:43,320
when you want to change something

20
00:00:43,320 --> 00:00:44,080
and you tell yourself

21
00:00:44,080 --> 00:00:47,120
you should have gone about it much earlier.

22
00:00:47,120 --> 00:00:50,560
Mathieu: Going back to school, learning French,

23
00:00:50,560 --> 00:00:52,690
or simply making room

24
00:00:52,690 --> 00:00:55,800
for an activity you'd like to get back to.

25
00:00:55,850 --> 00:00:57,900
Léa: If you're listening on the tram,

26
00:00:57,900 --> 00:01:00,120
at home or during your break,

27
00:01:00,120 --> 00:01:02,880
get as comfortable as you can.

28
00:01:02,880 --> 00:01:04,880
We're going to take our time.

29
00:01:04,880 --> 00:01:08,960
Mathieu: This conversation is for levels B1 and B2.

30
00:01:08,960 --> 00:01:09,600
By the end,

31
00:01:09,600 --> 00:01:13,440
you'll be able to talk about a project that matters to you

32
00:01:13,440 --> 00:01:16,520
and say what makes you hesitate.

33
00:01:16,520 --> 00:01:19,560
Léa: If a word escapes you, let us keep going.

34
00:01:19,560 --> 00:01:21,440
You can follow the story even

35
00:01:21,440 --> 00:01:23,240
if you don't understand everything.

36
00:01:23,290 --> 00:01:26,240
Mathieu: Say, were you thinking of a big change

37
00:01:26,240 --> 00:01:28,430
when you chose this topic?

38
00:01:28,480 --> 00:01:31,920
Léa: I was thinking of a wish that keeps coming back.

39
00:01:31,920 --> 00:01:32,550
For me,

40
00:01:32,550 --> 00:01:35,080
it came back every time I heard

41
00:01:35,080 --> 00:01:36,800
an interview on the radio.

42
00:01:36,800 --> 00:01:37,660
Mathieu: For me,

43
00:01:37,660 --> 00:01:42,040
every time my bike made a strange noise.

44
00:01:42,040 --> 00:01:43,360
Léa: We'll get to that.

45
00:01:43,360 --> 00:01:44,150
Mathieu: And you,

46
00:01:44,200 --> 00:01:47,720
if a wish comes to mind while we're talking,

47
00:01:47,720 --> 00:01:49,530
keep it in mind.

48
00:01:49,530 --> 00:01:52,720
Even if it seems a bit strange to you.

49
00:01:52,770 --> 00:01:55,720
Léa: With us, your wish is in good company.

50
00:01:55,720 --> 00:01:57,040
One Thursday in November,

51
00:01:57,040 --> 00:02:00,520
I stopped in front of the library window.

52
00:02:00,520 --> 00:02:03,520
I was waiting for the rain to ease off before going home.

53
00:02:03,520 --> 00:02:05,520
Mathieu: There are worse places to shelter.

54
00:02:05,520 --> 00:02:08,160
Léa: On the glass, there was a poster.

55
00:02:08,160 --> 00:02:11,110
The neighbourhood community radio was looking for

56
00:02:11,110 --> 00:02:13,840
volunteers to prepare reports.

57
00:02:13,840 --> 00:02:16,430
Mathieu: And you pushed the door open?

58
00:02:16,480 --> 00:02:18,240
Léa: I took a photo of the poster.

59
00:02:18,290 --> 00:02:20,400
Then I spent the journey home

60
00:02:20,400 --> 00:02:23,280
finding reasons to give up.

61
00:02:23,280 --> 00:02:25,310
Mathieu: How many did you find?

62
00:02:25,360 --> 00:02:27,160
Léa: Three before my stop.

63
00:02:27,160 --> 00:02:30,280
I had never touched a mixing desk.

64
00:02:30,280 --> 00:02:32,600
The others probably already knew each other.

65
00:02:32,600 --> 00:02:35,040
And I felt rusty at speaking.

66
00:02:35,040 --> 00:02:38,030
Mathieu: Rusty, like my bike chain?

67
00:02:38,080 --> 00:02:40,840
Léa: Yes. I had lost a bit of practice.

68
00:02:40,840 --> 00:02:44,080
At work, I mostly prepare written texts.

69
00:02:44,130 --> 00:02:47,320
Talking into a microphone with someone listening,

70
00:02:47,320 --> 00:02:48,880
that felt far away.

71
00:02:48,880 --> 00:02:51,110
Mathieu: Even when you know your subject?

72
00:02:51,160 --> 00:02:52,960
Léa: Especially when I know it.

73
00:02:52,960 --> 00:02:54,840
I want to explain everything,

74
00:02:54,840 --> 00:02:57,590
then I hear myself talking and I wonder

75
00:02:57,590 --> 00:02:59,320
where my sentence went.

76
00:02:59,320 --> 00:03:02,480
Mathieu: That happens even when you talk about cheese.

77
00:03:02,480 --> 00:03:04,240
Well, to me.

78
00:03:04,240 --> 00:03:05,090
Léa: On Sunday,

79
00:03:05,090 --> 00:03:08,280
I talked about it with my grandmother during our call.

80
00:03:08,280 --> 00:03:11,560
She asked me what I'd like to tell on the radio.

81
00:03:11,560 --> 00:03:13,640
Mathieu: Did you already have an idea?

82
00:03:13,690 --> 00:03:14,520
Léa: Right away.

83
00:03:14,520 --> 00:03:18,080
The bakery opens when the street is still empty.

84
00:03:18,080 --> 00:03:20,040
I wanted to ask the baker

85
00:03:20,040 --> 00:03:22,080
who her first customers are.

86
00:03:22,080 --> 00:03:23,920
Mathieu: I could be one of them.

87
00:03:23,920 --> 00:03:25,310
Léa: At six thirty?

88
00:03:25,360 --> 00:03:28,760
Mathieu: I could be one of them in spirit.

89
00:03:28,760 --> 00:03:31,440
Léa: I ended up sending a message to the radio.

90
00:03:31,440 --> 00:03:34,360
A volunteer suggested I come by one evening

91
00:03:34,360 --> 00:03:37,000
to see how they prepared the show.

92
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:38,950
Mathieu: And the famous bike?

93
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:40,460
Léa: The following week.

94
00:03:40,510 --> 00:03:43,150
I had come with the little bell from my front door

95
00:03:43,150 --> 00:03:46,480
to recreate the sound of the bakery door.

96
00:03:46,480 --> 00:03:49,430
Mathieu: Had you recorded the real door?

97
00:03:49,480 --> 00:03:52,400
Léa: Yes, but a lorry was going past at that moment.

98
00:03:52,400 --> 00:03:55,800
I tried to save the take with my little bell.

99
00:03:55,800 --> 00:03:59,080
In the studio, it sounded like a bicycle bell.

100
00:03:59,080 --> 00:04:01,350
Mathieu: And did it end up on air?

101
00:04:01,400 --> 00:04:02,640
Léa: Hold on to that question.

102
00:04:02,640 --> 00:04:05,160
I'll tell you what we put on air.

103
00:04:05,160 --> 00:04:07,880
Mathieu: Very well. I'll wait until the end of the show.

104
00:04:07,930 --> 00:04:11,480
Léa: That first evening, I mostly asked questions.

105
00:04:11,480 --> 00:04:13,360
When it was time to put on the headphones,

106
00:04:13,360 --> 00:04:15,920
I finally dared to say that I didn't know

107
00:04:15,920 --> 00:04:18,360
which side of the microphone to speak into.

108
00:04:18,360 --> 00:04:20,430
Mathieu: And someone showed you.

109
00:04:20,480 --> 00:04:22,560
Léa: In two seconds.

110
00:04:22,560 --> 00:04:25,520
Then he asked me if I was comfortable.

111
00:04:25,520 --> 00:04:27,830
I had prepared a whole explanation

112
00:04:27,830 --> 00:04:29,760
about my lack of experience.

113
00:04:29,760 --> 00:04:31,950
Mathieu: It stayed in your pocket.

114
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:33,920
Léa: With my phone, yes.

115
00:04:33,920 --> 00:04:35,700
I think about it when someone hesitates

116
00:04:35,700 --> 00:04:37,720
to speak up in French.

117
00:04:37,770 --> 00:04:40,560
Sometimes, the first sentence that counts

118
00:04:40,560 --> 00:04:42,590
is "Can you repeat that?"

119
00:04:42,640 --> 00:04:46,560
Mathieu: It lets the conversation carry on.

120
00:04:46,560 --> 00:04:50,040
And the other person knows what to do.

121
00:04:50,040 --> 00:04:51,030
As for me,

122
00:04:51,030 --> 00:04:55,000
I wanted to learn how to look after my bike.

123
00:04:55,000 --> 00:04:57,960
I walk past a community workshop

124
00:04:57,960 --> 00:05:00,600
almost every day on my way home.

125
00:05:00,600 --> 00:05:02,990
Léa: The one in the courtyard, near the little park?

126
00:05:03,040 --> 00:05:07,040
Mathieu: Yes. I used to watch the people behind the window.

127
00:05:07,090 --> 00:05:08,680
They'd take the tools,

128
00:05:08,680 --> 00:05:11,080
they'd put them back in the right place.

129
00:05:11,080 --> 00:05:14,040
Just that impressed me.

130
00:05:14,040 --> 00:05:16,350
Léa: Did you feel a bit out of place?

131
00:05:16,400 --> 00:05:19,050
Mathieu: I imagined walking in with my bike

132
00:05:19,050 --> 00:05:22,560
that squeaks and my glasses that slip.

133
00:05:22,560 --> 00:05:25,720
There was already a lot of noise in that scene.

134
00:05:25,720 --> 00:05:27,550
Léa: Mostly in your head.

135
00:05:27,600 --> 00:05:30,280
Mathieu: A lot. One evening,

136
00:05:30,280 --> 00:05:32,160
10 p.m. Mathieu

137
00:05:32,160 --> 00:05:35,760
decided to learn everything on the internet.

138
00:05:35,810 --> 00:05:38,720
He watched four videos in a row.

139
00:05:38,720 --> 00:05:40,560
Léa: He's brave, that one.

140
00:05:40,560 --> 00:05:43,440
Mathieu: Mostly he's very good at giving

141
00:05:43,440 --> 00:05:46,160
work to next-day Mathieu.

142
00:05:46,160 --> 00:05:47,200
The next day,

143
00:05:47,200 --> 00:05:51,040
I put on my shoes and set off on foot.

144
00:05:51,040 --> 00:05:53,750
Léa: What made you decide to step through the door?

145
00:05:53,800 --> 00:05:55,940
Mathieu: A volunteer opened the door

146
00:05:55,940 --> 00:05:58,720
while I was reading the opening hours.

147
00:05:58,720 --> 00:06:01,680
She asked me if I was looking for someone.

148
00:06:01,680 --> 00:06:03,920
Léa: And what did you answer?

149
00:06:03,970 --> 00:06:07,880
Mathieu: "I think I'm looking for where to put my bike."

150
00:06:07,880 --> 00:06:09,480
She showed me a spot

151
00:06:09,480 --> 00:06:12,120
and said she would come and see me.

152
00:06:12,120 --> 00:06:14,760
Léa: The first problem was solved.

153
00:06:14,760 --> 00:06:17,460
Mathieu: Exactly. The second one

154
00:06:17,460 --> 00:06:19,760
was that I had described my bike

155
00:06:19,760 --> 00:06:23,680
as "a bike that makes a noise".

156
00:06:23,680 --> 00:06:27,340
Even with a lot of goodwill,

157
00:06:27,340 --> 00:06:29,920
she needed a detail.

158
00:06:29,920 --> 00:06:31,760
Léa: Did you let her listen to it?

159
00:06:31,810 --> 00:06:34,960
Mathieu: We moved the bike into the courtyard.

160
00:06:34,960 --> 00:06:38,800
In front of her, complete silence.

161
00:06:38,800 --> 00:06:42,520
I almost wanted to tell her it was shy.

162
00:06:42,520 --> 00:06:44,480
Léa: It chose its moment.

163
00:06:44,480 --> 00:06:46,350
Mathieu: Luckily, she asked me

164
00:06:46,350 --> 00:06:49,920
in what conditions I heard the noise.

165
00:06:49,920 --> 00:06:51,170
I was able to show her

166
00:06:51,170 --> 00:06:53,680
what I was doing when it happened.

167
00:06:53,680 --> 00:06:55,910
Léa: And now, have you got the knack?

168
00:06:55,960 --> 00:07:00,640
Mathieu: For holding the bike while we looked at it together, yes.

169
00:07:00,690 --> 00:07:05,000
For everything else, I still need someone to show me.

170
00:07:05,000 --> 00:07:06,280
I'm fine with that.

171
00:07:06,280 --> 00:07:07,740
Léa: You gave yourself the right

172
00:07:07,740 --> 00:07:10,400
to come back with another question.

173
00:07:10,400 --> 00:07:13,720
Mathieu: I went back two weeks later.

174
00:07:13,720 --> 00:07:16,600
I recognised the volunteer and I was able to tell

175
00:07:16,600 --> 00:07:20,240
someone who was arriving where to put their bike.

176
00:07:20,240 --> 00:07:23,350
Léa: It's funny, the first thing you learned,

177
00:07:23,350 --> 00:07:25,840
you could already pass on.

178
00:07:25,840 --> 00:07:29,020
Mathieu: Yes. When you learn French,

179
00:07:29,020 --> 00:07:30,800
you sometimes get the impression that people

180
00:07:30,800 --> 00:07:34,560
who speak well have always been at ease.

181
00:07:34,560 --> 00:07:36,080
Léa: And in a group,

182
00:07:36,080 --> 00:07:37,800
sometimes all it takes is knowing

183
00:07:37,800 --> 00:07:40,760
a single sentence to welcome someone.

184
00:07:40,760 --> 00:07:43,560
"Come, there's a chair free here."

185
00:07:43,560 --> 00:07:46,800
Mathieu: Next time a group impresses you,

186
00:07:46,800 --> 00:07:50,640
watch how it welcomes someone who arrives.

187
00:07:50,640 --> 00:07:52,620
That will tell you more

188
00:07:52,620 --> 00:07:55,280
about it than everyone else's level.

189
00:07:55,330 --> 00:07:56,780
Léa: In both our stories,

190
00:07:56,780 --> 00:07:59,400
the timetables ended up working for us.

191
00:07:59,400 --> 00:08:01,000
I'm thinking of a friend,

192
00:08:01,000 --> 00:08:04,200
Samira, for whom it was more complicated.

193
00:08:04,200 --> 00:08:05,950
Mathieu: What did she want to do?

194
00:08:06,000 --> 00:08:08,000
Léa: She wanted to go back to a training course

195
00:08:08,000 --> 00:08:10,400
to work in a library.

196
00:08:10,400 --> 00:08:11,880
For several years,

197
00:08:11,880 --> 00:08:14,120
she had looked after a parent in the evenings,

198
00:08:14,120 --> 00:08:16,120
after work.

199
00:08:16,120 --> 00:08:18,480
When she was finally able to look into it,

200
00:08:18,480 --> 00:08:21,800
the course she had in mind had moved.

201
00:08:21,850 --> 00:08:22,860
With the commute,

202
00:08:22,860 --> 00:08:26,560
the evening classes became impossible for her.

203
00:08:26,560 --> 00:08:29,040
Mathieu: That must have been hard to take.

204
00:08:29,040 --> 00:08:30,880
Léa: Yes. She even found it hard

205
00:08:30,880 --> 00:08:33,400
to walk past the library again.

206
00:08:33,400 --> 00:08:36,640
She felt she had let her chance slip by.

207
00:08:36,640 --> 00:08:39,670
Mathieu: Did she manage to talk about it with someone?

208
00:08:39,720 --> 00:08:41,450
Léa: A colleague asked her

209
00:08:41,450 --> 00:08:44,600
which moment of the job attracted her most.

210
00:08:44,600 --> 00:08:47,480
Samira said she loved helping someone find

211
00:08:47,480 --> 00:08:49,840
a book they would really want to read.

212
00:08:49,890 --> 00:08:51,390
A few months later,

213
00:08:51,390 --> 00:08:54,010
the library looked for volunteers

214
00:08:54,010 --> 00:08:56,920
to take books to people's homes.

215
00:08:56,920 --> 00:08:59,290
She took on a round on Saturdays,

216
00:08:59,290 --> 00:09:00,760
once a month.

217
00:09:00,760 --> 00:09:03,430
Mathieu: And the idea of the training course?

218
00:09:03,480 --> 00:09:05,600
Léa: She still thinks about it.

219
00:09:05,600 --> 00:09:06,680
This year,

220
00:09:06,680 --> 00:09:10,120
her round gives her a link with that world

221
00:09:10,120 --> 00:09:13,040
and she's discovering what she enjoys.

222
00:09:13,040 --> 00:09:16,440
As for the rest, she needs time.

223
00:09:16,440 --> 00:09:19,440
Mathieu: Did she tell you about her first visit?

224
00:09:19,490 --> 00:09:23,760
Léa: Yes. A lady had asked for a detective novel.

225
00:09:23,760 --> 00:09:27,960
Samira expected to drop off the book and leave.

226
00:09:27,960 --> 00:09:31,200
The lady asked her to sit down.

227
00:09:31,200 --> 00:09:33,310
Mathieu: Did they talk about the novel?

228
00:09:33,360 --> 00:09:35,440
Léa: About the last one she had read.

229
00:09:35,440 --> 00:09:39,040
She had guessed the ending from the third chapter.

230
00:09:39,040 --> 00:09:40,290
For the next one,

231
00:09:40,290 --> 00:09:43,800
she wanted a story that would challenge her a bit.

232
00:09:43,800 --> 00:09:48,000
Mathieu: And Samira was able to choose the next book with her.

233
00:09:48,050 --> 00:09:50,840
Léa: Yes. When Samira told me about it,

234
00:09:50,840 --> 00:09:53,120
she remembered the title.

235
00:09:53,120 --> 00:09:55,760
She also remembered that the lady

236
00:09:55,760 --> 00:09:59,320
had cleared a chair before she arrived.

237
00:09:59,320 --> 00:10:00,650
Mathieu: There's something

238
00:10:00,650 --> 00:10:03,040
very concrete about that Saturday.

239
00:10:03,040 --> 00:10:07,360
Someone is waiting for a book, she arrives, they chat.

240
00:10:07,360 --> 00:10:10,640
Léa: And the following Saturday, she gets on with her life.

241
00:10:10,640 --> 00:10:12,860
She keeps the right to change her mind

242
00:10:12,860 --> 00:10:15,320
about what she wants to do later.

243
00:10:15,370 --> 00:10:19,360
Mathieu: In French, you can have the same kind of constraint.

244
00:10:19,360 --> 00:10:22,320
The only class that matches your level

245
00:10:22,320 --> 00:10:25,760
starts while you're still at work.

246
00:10:25,760 --> 00:10:28,270
Léa: A conversation at lunchtime can

247
00:10:28,270 --> 00:10:31,760
help you stay in touch with the language.

248
00:10:31,760 --> 00:10:33,380
And some weeks,

249
00:10:33,380 --> 00:10:36,840
you'll need to keep your break for yourself.

250
00:10:36,840 --> 00:10:39,030
Mathieu: If a project has to wait,

251
00:10:39,030 --> 00:10:40,510
what matters to you

252
00:10:40,510 --> 00:10:42,440
most in that project?

253
00:10:42,490 --> 00:10:46,960
Samira's answer fitted in one sentence.

254
00:10:46,960 --> 00:10:51,280
Léa: And that sentence gave her a place to look.

255
00:10:51,280 --> 00:10:52,920
Mathieu: To get back into a project,

256
00:10:52,920 --> 00:10:55,120
I like to start with a question

257
00:10:55,120 --> 00:10:58,480
that someone can answer precisely.

258
00:10:58,480 --> 00:11:00,510
Léa: In the case of the bike?

259
00:11:00,560 --> 00:11:03,910
Mathieu: "When exactly do you hear the noise?"

260
00:11:03,960 --> 00:11:07,640
I had already searched "how to fix a bike"

261
00:11:07,640 --> 00:11:11,410
and ended up with fourteen tabs open.

262
00:11:11,460 --> 00:11:13,110
Léa: Fourteen?

263
00:11:13,160 --> 00:11:14,940
Mathieu: 10 p.m. Mathieu

264
00:11:14,940 --> 00:11:17,520
works on several files at once.

265
00:11:17,520 --> 00:11:18,960
For a training course,

266
00:11:18,960 --> 00:11:21,640
I'd ask if there's another date.

267
00:11:21,640 --> 00:11:22,960
For French,

268
00:11:22,960 --> 00:11:25,840
I'd ask someone at what time

269
00:11:25,840 --> 00:11:28,120
they'd be free to chat.

270
00:11:28,120 --> 00:11:30,880
Léa: With a timetable, it takes shape.

271
00:11:30,880 --> 00:11:35,160
And when things get stuck, you know what to think about.

272
00:11:35,160 --> 00:11:36,710
Do you remember the person

273
00:11:36,710 --> 00:11:38,640
who welcomed you at the workshop?

274
00:11:38,690 --> 00:11:41,970
If she had just given you the list of tools,

275
00:11:41,970 --> 00:11:43,430
would you have come back?

276
00:11:43,480 --> 00:11:44,960
Mathieu: I don't know.

277
00:11:44,960 --> 00:11:46,680
Above all, I needed to see

278
00:11:46,680 --> 00:11:50,200
where I'd spend the evening and with whom.

279
00:11:50,200 --> 00:11:54,360
Léa: You can ask to visit the room before signing up.

280
00:11:54,360 --> 00:11:58,020
Mathieu: Yes. Meeting the person who runs the class

281
00:11:58,020 --> 00:12:00,760
also helps you get an idea.

282
00:12:00,760 --> 00:12:02,820
Personally, I waited a long time

283
00:12:02,820 --> 00:12:06,560
because I thought I had to choose right away.

284
00:12:06,610 --> 00:12:08,190
Léa: I also find it helps

285
00:12:08,190 --> 00:12:10,760
to know how long you're going to try.

286
00:12:10,760 --> 00:12:11,510
At the radio,

287
00:12:11,510 --> 00:12:12,340
I told myself

288
00:12:12,340 --> 00:12:15,440
that I'd spend two evenings with the team.

289
00:12:15,440 --> 00:12:17,840
Then I'd take stock.

290
00:12:17,840 --> 00:12:20,110
Mathieu: And you could have stopped there.

291
00:12:20,160 --> 00:12:23,400
Léa: Of course. When something matters to you,

292
00:12:23,400 --> 00:12:25,940
you can make a little room for it

293
00:12:25,940 --> 00:12:29,040
and see what that room brings you.

294
00:12:29,040 --> 00:12:31,680
Mathieu: Do you have an example with French?

295
00:12:31,730 --> 00:12:33,900
Léa: Someone who loves films can follow

296
00:12:33,900 --> 00:12:37,720
a discussion about a single film they already know.

297
00:12:37,720 --> 00:12:39,860
They have a story to tell,

298
00:12:39,860 --> 00:12:42,040
even if they're missing some words.

299
00:12:42,040 --> 00:12:44,180
Mathieu: And they can come out of the discussion

300
00:12:44,180 --> 00:12:47,480
having just picked up the name of another film.

301
00:12:47,480 --> 00:12:49,200
That counts too.

302
00:12:49,200 --> 00:12:50,470
Léa: Yes. At the beginning,

303
00:12:50,470 --> 00:12:54,920
I listened to my report with only one idea in mind.

304
00:12:54,920 --> 00:12:56,500
Did my voice sound

305
00:12:56,500 --> 00:12:58,670
like the voices of radio people?

306
00:12:58,720 --> 00:13:00,320
Mathieu: And now?

307
00:13:00,370 --> 00:13:03,720
Léa: I wonder if you can hear the baker clearly.

308
00:13:03,720 --> 00:13:06,220
She told me that a gentleman sometimes came

309
00:13:06,220 --> 00:13:09,640
to get his bread wearing his slippers.

310
00:13:09,640 --> 00:13:13,520
Mathieu: I'd like to point out that my slippers stay at home.

311
00:13:13,520 --> 00:13:15,800
Léa: I didn't give any names.

312
00:13:15,800 --> 00:13:19,310
Mathieu: Right, can we talk about the little bell now?

313
00:13:19,360 --> 00:13:20,140
Léa: In the edit,

314
00:13:20,140 --> 00:13:23,120
I put back the recording of the real door,

315
00:13:23,120 --> 00:13:24,880
lorry included.

316
00:13:24,880 --> 00:13:28,840
You hear the baker say hello right after.

317
00:13:28,890 --> 00:13:30,760
It gives her voice its full place.

318
00:13:30,760 --> 00:13:33,240
Mathieu: So the lorry made it onto the radio.

319
00:13:33,240 --> 00:13:37,120
Léa: And it knew nothing about its first report.

320
00:13:37,120 --> 00:13:38,840
Mathieu: I like that detail.

321
00:13:38,840 --> 00:13:41,080
The noise you wanted to erase

322
00:13:41,080 --> 00:13:44,240
is the beginning of the story as it really happened.

323
00:13:44,240 --> 00:13:45,840
Léa: For those of you listening,

324
00:13:45,840 --> 00:13:49,600
what could you leave a little imperfect

325
00:13:49,600 --> 00:13:53,630
so your project moves forward at your own pace?

326
00:13:53,680 --> 00:13:56,680
Mathieu: There's something we haven't said yet.

327
00:13:56,730 --> 00:13:57,560
You can take the plunge

328
00:13:57,560 --> 00:14:00,010
and still feel like giving up

329
00:14:00,010 --> 00:14:01,760
after two weeks.

330
00:14:01,760 --> 00:14:04,030
Léa: Did that happen to you with the workshop?

331
00:14:04,080 --> 00:14:06,640
Mathieu: I missed three opening sessions in a row.

332
00:14:06,640 --> 00:14:08,400
Once, I had work.

333
00:14:08,400 --> 00:14:10,960
Another time, I had promised to see my sister.

334
00:14:10,960 --> 00:14:15,120
The third time, I went home with some Comté.

335
00:14:15,120 --> 00:14:18,040
Léa: The Comté needed some company.

336
00:14:18,040 --> 00:14:19,920
Mathieu: I sacrificed myself.

337
00:14:19,920 --> 00:14:21,720
But the fourth week,

338
00:14:21,720 --> 00:14:22,670
I wondered

339
00:14:22,670 --> 00:14:24,600
if I was going to look ridiculous

340
00:14:24,650 --> 00:14:27,040
coming back with the same question.

341
00:14:27,040 --> 00:14:29,150
Léa: And did you see the volunteer again?

342
00:14:29,200 --> 00:14:31,700
Mathieu: Yes. She asked me to hold a lamp

343
00:14:31,700 --> 00:14:33,640
while she was looking for a screw.

344
00:14:33,640 --> 00:14:36,960
Five minutes later, we were talking about my bike again.

345
00:14:36,960 --> 00:14:39,720
Léa: Time had passed for her too.

346
00:14:39,720 --> 00:14:43,280
She had bikes to look at, screws to find.

347
00:14:43,280 --> 00:14:45,920
She wasn't there counting your absences.

348
00:14:45,920 --> 00:14:47,800
Mathieu: That did me good.

349
00:14:47,800 --> 00:14:50,160
When you've drifted away from an activity,

350
00:14:50,210 --> 00:14:51,910
you forget that the people in it

351
00:14:51,910 --> 00:14:54,960
keep having ordinary days.

352
00:14:54,960 --> 00:14:56,670
If you feel rusty

353
00:14:56,670 --> 00:14:59,240
in French after a long break,

354
00:14:59,240 --> 00:15:02,640
you can go back to a voice you already know.

355
00:15:02,640 --> 00:15:04,780
Personally, I'd replay an episode

356
00:15:04,780 --> 00:15:06,960
whose topic I remember.

357
00:15:06,960 --> 00:15:09,020
The pleasure of recognising a word

358
00:15:09,020 --> 00:15:11,320
can be enough for today.

359
00:15:11,320 --> 00:15:15,120
Léa: And if you recognise several, enjoy it.

360
00:15:15,120 --> 00:15:18,520
You're allowed to find that pleasant.

361
00:15:18,570 --> 00:15:19,530
Mathieu, you said

362
00:15:19,530 --> 00:15:22,080
that you had the knack of holding your bike

363
00:15:22,080 --> 00:15:24,080
while we were looking at the wheel.

364
00:15:24,080 --> 00:15:25,870
Mathieu: To have the knack

365
00:15:25,870 --> 00:15:27,190
is to know how to do something with ease

366
00:15:27,190 --> 00:15:30,240
because you're used to it.

367
00:15:30,240 --> 00:15:33,520
At first, I folded napkins crooked.

368
00:15:33,520 --> 00:15:35,760
Now, I've got the knack.

369
00:15:35,760 --> 00:15:38,470
Léa: Do you want to come to dinner at my place on Saturday?

370
00:15:38,520 --> 00:15:40,960
Mathieu: I can see exactly what you're doing.

371
00:15:40,960 --> 00:15:42,680
We also talked about a project

372
00:15:42,680 --> 00:15:44,240
that matters to us.

373
00:15:44,240 --> 00:15:47,200
It means it counts a lot for us.

374
00:15:47,250 --> 00:15:48,640
For example,

375
00:15:48,640 --> 00:15:52,320
visiting my former teacher matters to me.

376
00:15:52,320 --> 00:15:55,320
He encouraged me when I needed it.

377
00:15:55,320 --> 00:15:57,120
Léa: In front of the radio poster,

378
00:15:57,120 --> 00:15:59,260
I had already found three reasons

379
00:15:59,260 --> 00:16:02,120
to give up on my report idea.

380
00:16:02,120 --> 00:16:03,330
Mathieu: To give up

381
00:16:03,330 --> 00:16:05,650
means to stop dealing with something

382
00:16:05,650 --> 00:16:07,920
or to abandon a project.

383
00:16:07,920 --> 00:16:10,320
I dropped my entry for the tournament

384
00:16:10,320 --> 00:16:12,120
when I saw the times.

385
00:16:12,120 --> 00:16:14,840
Léa: You can also say "forget it"

386
00:16:14,890 --> 00:16:17,480
when a friend is looking for something for you

387
00:16:17,480 --> 00:16:19,520
and you've just found it.

388
00:16:19,520 --> 00:16:21,650
Mathieu: Often with my glasses.

389
00:16:21,650 --> 00:16:24,320
Most of the time, they're on my head.

390
00:16:24,320 --> 00:16:25,600
Léa: And to take stock

391
00:16:25,600 --> 00:16:29,240
is to stop for a moment to see where you are.

392
00:16:29,240 --> 00:16:31,960
After three months in her new flat,

393
00:16:31,960 --> 00:16:34,920
Julie took stock of her spending.

394
00:16:34,920 --> 00:16:36,600
Mathieu: I thought you were going to

395
00:16:36,600 --> 00:16:39,320
talk about my cheese spending.

396
00:16:39,320 --> 00:16:43,400
Léa: I promised a twenty-minute show.

397
00:16:43,450 --> 00:16:47,160
Mathieu: Very well. And the last word is "rusty".

398
00:16:47,160 --> 00:16:49,840
We use it when we've lost a bit of ease

399
00:16:49,840 --> 00:16:52,480
because we haven't practised for a long time.

400
00:16:52,480 --> 00:16:53,480
Léa: For example,

401
00:16:53,480 --> 00:16:56,690
I still know how to use a sewing machine,

402
00:16:56,690 --> 00:16:59,040
but I'm a bit rusty.

403
00:16:59,040 --> 00:17:00,410
The last time,

404
00:17:00,410 --> 00:17:03,400
it took me ages to thread the needle.

405
00:17:03,400 --> 00:17:06,800
Mathieu: With a bit of practice, some movements come back.

406
00:17:06,800 --> 00:17:10,960
And sometimes, we ask someone to show us again.

407
00:17:11,010 --> 00:17:12,590
Léa: Which often gives us

408
00:17:12,590 --> 00:17:16,040
a more interesting conversation than expected.

409
00:17:16,040 --> 00:17:18,800
Thank you for spending this time with us.

410
00:17:18,800 --> 00:17:21,680
If a wish came back to you while listening to us,

411
00:17:21,680 --> 00:17:23,010
you can tell us

412
00:17:23,010 --> 00:17:25,360
what draws you to that project.

413
00:17:25,360 --> 00:17:29,020
Mathieu: I'd love to know what you'd like to discover,

414
00:17:29,020 --> 00:17:32,040
even if you don't know where to start yet.

415
00:17:32,040 --> 00:17:33,970
Léa: Subscribe to The Cozy French

416
00:17:33,970 --> 00:17:37,840
if you want more of our conversations in French.

417
00:17:37,890 --> 00:17:41,360
We'll be happy to see you in the next episode.

418
00:17:41,360 --> 00:17:42,640
Mathieu: Take care of yourselves.

419
00:17:42,640 --> 00:17:45,670
And, Léa, what happened to the little bell?

420
00:17:45,720 --> 00:17:47,600
Léa: I put it back on my door.

421
00:17:47,600 --> 00:17:49,320
It's doing its job again.

422
00:17:49,320 --> 00:17:52,230
Mathieu: Ah. And if one day it wants a career change,

423
00:17:52,230 --> 00:17:54,520
the workshop is open on Thursdays.

424
00:17:54,520 --> 00:17:57,000
Léa: It'll take stock first.

425
00:17:57,000 --> 00:17:59,080
See you soon, everyone!
